WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.110 align:start position:0%
 
all of us  at one time or another  find ourselves in a wilderness  we find that we have left familiar  territory  the way ahead is unfamiliar  difficult  uncomfortable  maybe even painful  the wilderness  may be an environmental wilderness  as when mountain climbers are  unexpectedly caught in a  blizzard or when hikers take a wrong  turn and are lost  it may be an emotional wilderness  as when married couples go through a  divorce  or a loved one dies  it may be a psychological wilderness  as when depression descends  or schizophrenia makes itself known  it may be a spiritual wilderness as when  a faithful person suddenly loses faith  or a prayer goes unanswered for a long  time  it may be a medical wilderness as when a  person is diagnosed with a serious  illness  or when a chronic illness takes hold of  a life  it may be a situational wilderness as  when a young adult goes off to college  for the first time  or when someone is laid off and finds  himself jobless  whatever kind of wilderness it is  it is a place and time in which we feel  lost  alone  powerless  the reading from numbers  describes just such a place and time in  the lives of the hebrew people  moses had delivered them from slavery in  egypt and they were journeying in the  wilderness to the promised land  but it was a long  and difficult journey  at times they were thirsty  at times they were hungry  at times they were tired  at times they faced enemies  and at times they were just plain fed up  with the whole  thing that's the way they felt in the  reading for today  they were just plain fed up with it  they turned to their leader moses and  said why why have you brought us out of  egypt to die in the wilderness  for there's no food and there's no water  and we detest this miserable food  in the midst of the challenges of the  journey which were many  they'd forgotten how horrible their  lives were in egypt  they'd forgotten that they were slaves  they'd forgotten that god went to a lot  of trouble to free them  they'd forgotten that moses left a  comfortable life to become their  liberator he was married  he was a shepherd he was  nice it was nice he didn't want to go  all they remembered  is that in egypt they had had food to  eat  and a place to rest their heads  and the truth is  they still had food to eat and a place  to rest their heads in the wilderness  when they had been thirsty god had  provided them with water  when they had been hungry god had  provided food  when they had been tired  god had provided protection  when they had faced enemies  god had defended them  god had met  their  needs  he was even meeting their needs in this  passage he was providing them with food  it just happened to be food they didn't  like  they called it miserable food  well god got a little fed up in return  think of how you felt when you've gone  above and beyond the call of duty to  help someone and no matter what you've  done or how hard you've tried to help it  wasn't enough  the person didn't appreciate your  efforts and continued along their  miserable way complaining  i relate to moses in this passage in my  role as a parent  and i apologize to my children who are  now sitting out there  but there are times  when it seems like i have done  everything i can to take care of my  children and it is still not enough  the food i've prepared isn't the right  food  the clothes i've washed aren't their  favorites  the item i've purchased is the wrong  item  the play dates i've set up aren't enough  play dates  eventually i've gotten fed up with their  complaints and whining and i have read  them the riot act  well i can imagine that that is how god  felt when the hebrew people complained  this time god had had enough  hadn't god been working hard to free  them from slavery and lead them safely  to the promised land  it was time they realized just how good  they had it  so  god sent those poisonous snakes to teach  them a lesson  and once the snakes arrived people  shaped up pretty fast  they went to moses and they said oh we  have sinned by speaking against the lord  and against you pray to the lord to take  away the serpents from us  well of course  moses did as the people asked  god responded by telling moses to make a  poisonous snake and put it on a pole  when a snake bit someone  all the person had to do was look at  that snake made by moses and he or she  would live  now to us in this day and age that  sounds pretty strange  i mean how could that work  but in addition to sounding strange it's  also fascinating  think about it  the thing that in one instance caused  death  in another gave life  the snake could either destroy  or heal it could either kill  or save  what does it mean  for us today  that in one instance a poisonous snake  caused death  and that in another it gave life  in order to explore the meaning of this  dual effect of the poisonous snake we  have to go back to the discussion of  wildernesses with which i opened this  sermon  as i said all of us at one time or  another will find ourselves in a  wilderness of some sort  now wildernesses are difficult  because  they are unfamiliar territory  the coping devices  we've created over the years don't  necessarily work in the wilderness  the wilderness territory may require  different kinds of coping devices ones  we haven't yet developed so we're left  feeling very vulnerable  fears and anxieties that we can manage  in familiar territory become  unmanageable in the wilderness  feeling badly  may lead to behaving badly  as it did with the hebrew people on  their way to the promised land  now these fears and anxieties that  become unmanageable in the wildernesses  of our lives are like the snakes in the  passage from numbers  if we let them get the better of us  they poison us and eventually kill us  now they may not kill us literally but  they may kill us figuratively  they may kill our feelings or our spirit  or our mind  they may make us into the living dead  zombies are very popular now in popular  culture so think of that  they may keep us  in the wilderness forever  but if we don't let them get the better  of us they become the vehicles by which  we are healed  they make us into the living living they  lead us out of the wilderness  now the thing that determined whether  the snakes were agents of death or of  life for the hebrew people was the  nature of their relationship to god  when they turned away from god the snake  was an agent of death  when they turned toward god the snake  was an agent of life  it stands to reason then that the thing  that determines whether our fears and  anxieties are agents of death or of life  for us  is the nature of our relationship to god  when we turn away from god our fears and  anxieties can overtake us  and become agents of death  but when we turn toward god  our fears and anxieties can become the  vehicles by which god heals us  they become agents of life  now you might be wondering  what that would look like  so i'm going to give you an example of  what i mean  some of you might be familiar with the  writings of anne lamott  she's a poet  a novelist and a memoirist  i don't know if that's a real word but  i'm using it  who writes about her spiritual journey  in her book traveling mercies she writes  about the death of her best friend pami  and the deep  dark grief into which it sent her  this is what she wrote about her  struggle with huge and monolithic grief  the more often i cried and felt just  generally wretched  the more often i started to have  occasional moments of utter joy  of feeling aware of each moment shining  for its own momentous sake  don't get me wrong  grief is awful  it really is  unfortunately though avoiding it robs us  of life of the now of a sense of living  spirit  the bad news is that whatever you use to  keep the pain at bay robs you of the  flecks and nuggets of gold that feeling  grief will give you  finally  grief ends up giving you the two best  things  softness  and illumination  a few months before pammy died we read a  line by the great persian mystical poet  rumi  where there is ruin  there is hope for a treasure  anne  devastated by the death of her dearest  friend in the world entered the  wilderness of grief  in that wilderness she discovered the  snake of her fear of grief  had anne given into her fear and refused  to feel the grief that was inside of her  she would have become the living dead  in the process of cutting off her  feeling of grief she would have cut off  all sorts of other feelings  she would have been as if dead  stuck in that wilderness  instead she didn't give in to her fear  she understood that she had to face her  fear and turn toward her grief  just as the hebrew people had had to  turn toward that bronze serpent and  allow god to use it to heal her  and accepted the ruin of her grief  trusting that god was with her in it and  discovered the treasure of softness  and illumination  she just discovered the gift of god's  grace that is ever at work in our lives  even  in the wilderness  now anne's wilderness was grief  but there are all kinds of wildernesses  the point is not what kind of wilderness  it is but what we do when we find  ourselves in one  do we resist the wilderness experience  try to control or manage it so that we  don't have to suffer  or do we surrender to it  and move into it despite the suffering  trusting in the grace and power of god  to get us to the promised land  in other words do we get stuck  for fear of suffering  or do we allow suffering to move us  along  in our spiritual journey  recently i was reminded of this choice  between staying stuck in misery  or journeying to the promised land while  i was having a conversation with my  sister and she's given me permission to  repeat it  here we were talking about spiritual  growth and she shared with me with me  the story of giving birth to her son 28  years before  he's here today  she had taken all of the classes one is  supposed to take before giving birth  class is about what labor will look and  feel like and how to manage the pain  that is of course an inevitable part of  the labor process  so of course while giving birth  she was careful to manage the pain with  all the techniques she had learned  well this went on for about five hours  without the labor progressing  and that's not good  finally the midwife who was attending  her said  do you want to have this baby  and my sister of course said yes  the midwife then said  the next time you have a contraction  find where it hurts  and make it hurt  more  to which my sister replied probably with  some disbelief and indignation you have  got to be kidding i am doing labor  correctly i'm managing the pain and i'm  controlling the situation what do you  mean find where it hurts and make it  hurt more  the midwife having been through this  many times with many women stood her  ground and repeated if you want to have  this baby  find where it hurts  and make it hurt more  well my sister reluctantly followed her  advice  the labor went smoothly and my nephew  was born without complications and sits  here with us today  in the wilderness of labor  my sister was faced with staying stuck  in the misery of the process or moving  deeper into the pain in order to get to  the other side of it  had she decided to stay stuck both she  and my nephew would surely have died  fortunately she made the wise decision  however reluctant of embracing the pain  so that my nephew could be born and she  could live  all of us  at one time or another  will find ourselves in a wilderness  the way ahead will be unfamiliar  difficult  uncomfortable  maybe even painful  along the way  we will encounter serpents of fear  and anxiety  but the very things that create in us  fear and anxiety are the very things  that god will use to heal us and lead us  as we walk along the frightening and  unfamiliar terrain we must remember that  god created resurrection out of  crucifixion  that god could only raise jesus from the  tomb after  he was lifted up on the cross  we must remember that neither death  nor life  nor angels  nor rulers nor things present nor things  to come  nor powers nor height  nor depth  nor anything else in all creation  will be able to separate us from the  love of god in christ jesus our lord  god will not abandon us in our  wildernesses  god will not allow our snakes to  overtake us as long as we lift them up  to him  and allow him to transform them into  agents of healing  and givers of life  amen

